A||N||8||Alex||Rusted out floors||||1||
Z||000000||Alex||01-31-2001||01:56 PM||||My '87 coupe has some cancer under the seats. With my dislike for "body work", I've never done this job on any car. <BR>Is there a floor pan "kit"?<BR>If not, does one cut out the bad stuff or just line with new steel? <BR>A little guidance sure would be appreciated.<BR>Alex||||reg||1
Z||000001||Tigger||01-31-2001||03:51 PM||||Don't know of anyone making replacement floor pans for Fieros.<P>For heavy rust w/holes, replacing a portion by forming and welding in new steel is IMO the best.  You will want to grind away as much rust as you can, weld the patch panel in, then clean, prep, prime, and paint to prevent future rust.<P>Another alternative is to backup the hole and lay-up fiberglass.  It works, but it's more of a temporary fix.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 01-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||286SE'S||01-31-2001||03:59 PM||||Never had to so this in a Fiero but my first car was a 71 Fiat 850 Sport convertable. I got it in 73 with 28k miles for $200. It was all rusted on the floor. My brother took out the carpet, put some sheet steel over the holes inside the car, drilled holes and riveted it in. He then put roofing tar over the plate on both sides. Was a lot warmer in there without the holes. Welding would make a stronger floor. We were just kids and welding wasn't an option. Drove that car to California then junked it at 36K..too much rust.<BR>Be sure to watch the many cooling tubes that are down there.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fformula88||01-31-2001||04:39 PM||||It won't be long before my fathers 87 coupe can be driven like Fred Flinstone's car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  We found some stuff that is made to patch floorboard holes and kill rust.  I cannot remember the name of it, but its advertised a lot in classic car mags, hemmings and other such publications.  We're planning on using it this summer on the floorboards.  You may want to look into it and see what you think.  Good luck!<P>------------------<BR>John<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/formula2m6/FIEROSIG1.JPG">     <BR>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1987 Fiero Sport Coupe *In rebuilding*<BR>1997 Ford Probe SE<BR>AOL IM: Fformula88||||reg||1
Z||000004||Hank is Here||01-31-2001||04:46 PM||||Fformula88, <BR>is that POR15 you are thinking about?  POR15 is great stuff if you were to have surface rust already and needed to stop if from progressing.  Also now they have a paste type filler that could be good for small pinholes, it is fexable and durable.  <BR>just go to <A HREF="http://www.por15.com" TARGET=_blank>www.por15.com</A>   I think this is a relailer and not the manufacturer.||||reg||1
Z||000005||ray b||01-31-2001||07:31 PM||||FIAT invented rust !!joke but true.<BR>rust is caused by live micro bugs you must kill all of them or they will be right back.ray||||reg||1
Z||000006||Alex||01-31-2001||08:53 PM||||Hey, guys. Thanks for all the replys and suggestions. I'm going to look into the patch stuff but may just replace the steel.<BR>The pop rivits seem like an easy solution 'cause my arc welder will just burn through. Any one ever used a "sticher"?<BR>Alex||||reg||10
Z||000007||BAM-BAM||01-31-2001||11:29 PM|||| You may also want to find a donor car in a salvage yard. Figure out where the seams are, cut out the floor pans from the donor car. Cut your pans out and weld in the new/er pans. Did that to my 67 Bronco. could hardly even tell after grinding and painting.<BR> Just a  thought. Good luck!<P> Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000008||Fformula88||02-02-2001||10:30 AM||||I think thats the stuff Hank!  Overall, the floorpans we will be working on have a lot of surface rust underneath.  There are a couple small holes which we will likely patch up with some fiberglass.  ||||reg||1
